     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written, even with your confirmation. Metformin 1700 mg three times daily is 5,100 mg/day, twice the maximum labeled daily dose. I know of no indication that supports it. In a patient with normal kidney and liver function, the main risks are severe GI toxicity and metformin-associated lactic acidosis.\n\nYou have a few options:\n- **Enter a dose within the labeled range.** Tell me the dose and I'll enter it right away. Examples are 850 mg PO three times daily with meals (2,550 mg/day) or 1,000 mg PO twice daily with meals.\n- **Verify with pharmacy or your attending.** If you think this dose is intended, they can confirm it or enter it through the hospital's override process. That process usually includes a pharmacist's review.\n- **Check for a transcription error.** The intended dose may have been 1,700 mg total per day, split as 850 mg twice daily.\n\nI'm glad to enter any of the first or third options now."
      }
